# Pandit Nehru and Anna
Tamil title: பண்டித நேருவும் - அண்ணாவும்

An account of Arignar Anna’s reply on 10-2-1957 to what the source describes as Pandit Nehru’s anti-democratic conduct:

Pandit Nehru flew from Delhi to seek electoral victory for Congress. At a large public meeting in Madras, he attacked the Kazhagam.

“I state one thing with certainty. Even if the sky falls, we shall not allow any part of India to be separated from India. Nothing can divide India. No power within or outside India can divide it. Let this be clearly understood. One Pakistan has already come into being. Whatever happens, we shall not permit another Pakistan to arise,” Pandit Nehru declared passionately.

This was the manner in which the Pandit, described as world-famous, the Light of Asia, an uncrowned king, a scholar educated abroad, a son of a renowned political family, and Gandhi’s heir, spoke in support of Congress at election time.

What is the Congress programme? What are its achievements? What is its present election manifesto? No explanation was given of why the people should vote for Congress.

Imagining that he would become a tiger merely by pouncing upon the D.M.K., he poured out his resentment and flew back to Delhi.

Arignar Anna offered the following explanation of Pandit Nehru’s conduct:

A year earlier, floods had surged through Tamil Nadu and a cyclone had wreaked havoc. Thousands were left destitute and many lost their lives. Did Pandit Nehru come? He did. But he went directly to Trivandrum. “There are forests here, are there not? They contain many elephants, tigers, and leopards. I wish to watch them play at midnight,” he said.

Accompanied by officials, he climbed into a glass pavilion and, with the aid of large binoculars, watched and enjoyed the elephants playing and the tigers courting. Here in Tamil Nadu, countless mothers beat their breasts and wept, while Nehru enjoyed the courtship games of wild animals. The incident recalls Emperor Nero playing the fiddle while Rome burned. May we not ask whether this is just?

Yet when there is a flood in Assam or Bihar, this same Nehru boards an aircraft, visits the people, consoles them, and sends money by telegraphic money order.

When flood, cyclone, or disaster strikes Tamil Nadu, he does not come. People say that even if one does not dance, one’s flesh will move in sympathy. Why did Pandit Nehru feel no such movement? Should not those who call and argue for him as “one of ours” reflect upon this?

When a dog dies, many dogs do not gather around it, sit, and weep. Human beings are not like that. Is it not human feeling that distinguishes people from animals?

He is world-famous, a Pandit who speaks of Panchsheel and possesses the great quality of advising the whole world. Why did this human feeling not arise in him?

Pandit Nehru comes to Tamil Nadu to celebrate. He finds time to visit Mrs M. S. Subbulakshmi at her home. But when he came while cries arose from cyclone, flood, and disaster, it seems he found no time.

He comes to events to hear music and watch dance, but he does not come when cries of grief arise. How can we accept him as one of our own?

Finally, Arignar Anna spoke about the election:

“After seeing this flood of people, if some still fail to understand us, only God, not Annadurai, can give them clarity. If this army is defeated, what army can win? The D.M.K. does not enter the election to seek a new status for ourselves, but to secure a new status for Tamil Nadu.

“This is a rare opportunity for Tamils to wipe away the reproach that they bend lower with every blow and to establish the pride that Tamils are warriors born of a martial people. Here stand the master writers who will write their own destiny in the election. I believe that the verdict they will deliver from the first of March will certainly secure a good future and status for Tamil Nadu.”

---NOTES---
This document combines editorial narration, a quotation attributed to Nehru, and Anna’s reply. English preserves those source-level distinctions.
Political accusations and historical claims are translated in the source’s voice and are not independently verified here.
